1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,130
Every support team has a knowledge base.

2
00:00:03,130 --> 00:00:08,590
And every knowledge base has the same quiet
problem: it only knows what somebody

3
00:00:08,590 --> 00:00:10,091
thought to write down.

4
00:00:10,091 --> 00:00:11,934
Whatever it was never told,

5
00:00:11,934 --> 00:00:16,848
it simply can't answer — no matter how many
times the question comes in.

6
00:00:18,148 --> 00:00:20,563
Here's what actually happens today.

7
00:00:20,563 --> 00:00:25,117
Someone answers the question by hand, from
memory, under deadline.

8
00:00:25,117 --> 00:00:28,705
It's never written down anywhere the system
can use.

9
00:00:28,705 --> 00:00:29,905
So next week,

10
00:00:29,905 --> 00:00:35,426
a different customer asks the exact same
thing — and somebody answers it by hand

11
00:00:35,426 --> 00:00:36,626
again.

12
00:00:36,626 --> 00:00:38,509
Twenty minutes, every repeat, forever.

13
00:00:39,459 --> 00:00:40,659
This is EverMind.

14
00:00:40,659 --> 00:00:45,240
It reads the log of questions your knowledge
base couldn't answer,

15
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:50,863
and does the first pass on every one of them
— so a person sitting down to review

16
00:00:50,863 --> 00:00:53,987
ends up looking at a short list of
decisions,

17
00:00:53,987 --> 00:00:56,972
not a long list of raw, unsorted questions.

18
00:00:58,252 --> 00:01:00,774
Here's the whole idea in one picture.

19
00:01:00,774 --> 00:01:02,887
Questions come in from the log.

20
00:01:02,887 --> 00:01:05,204
Similar ones get grouped together,

21
00:01:05,204 --> 00:01:10,725
so five people asking the same thing in five
different ways becomes one decision,

22
00:01:10,725 --> 00:01:11,925
not five.

23
00:01:11,925 --> 00:01:15,401
Every group gets sorted into one of three
outcomes.

24
00:01:15,401 --> 00:01:18,809
A genuine gap gets drafted with a source
attached.

25
00:01:18,809 --> 00:01:23,648
And the only way anything crosses into your
knowledge base is a person,

26
00:01:23,648 --> 00:01:25,183
on the right, signing off.

27
00:01:26,133 --> 00:01:29,202
This question had never been answered
before.

28
00:01:29,202 --> 00:01:33,021
EverMind found it, checked the trusted source
documents,

29
00:01:33,021 --> 00:01:37,795
and drafted an answer — with the exact
document it came from attached,

30
00:01:37,795 --> 00:01:43,115
so a reviewer can verify it in seconds
instead of researching it from scratch.

31
00:01:44,415 --> 00:01:46,083
This one only looks new.

32
00:01:46,083 --> 00:01:51,923
It's the same question your knowledge base
already answers, just worded differently.

33
00:01:51,923 --> 00:01:56,580
EverMind recognizes the match and stops there
— no wasted drafting,

34
00:01:56,580 --> 00:02:02,003
and no duplicate entry cluttering the
knowledge base for your team to clean up

35
00:02:02,003 --> 00:02:02,770
later.

36
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:07,688
And some questions simply aren't support
questions at all.

37
00:02:07,688 --> 00:02:10,903
This one's about a job opening, not a
shipment.

38
00:02:10,903 --> 00:02:12,887
EverMind recognizes that too,

39
00:02:12,887 --> 00:02:18,497
and declines before it ever gets near
drafting — it knows the difference between a

40
00:02:18,497 --> 00:02:23,560
gap in the knowledge base and a question that
was never its job to answer.

41
00:02:24,860 --> 00:02:26,936
Before writing a line of code,

42
00:02:26,936 --> 00:02:31,987
we wrote down exactly what this system must
do — and just as importantly,

43
00:02:31,987 --> 00:02:37,245
what it must never do — and had that reviewed
before the build even started.

44
00:02:37,245 --> 00:02:42,988
Same discipline any engineering organization
uses before committing real resources:

45
00:02:42,988 --> 00:02:45,341
agree the requirements in writing,

46
00:02:45,341 --> 00:02:48,455
then build to them, not the other way
around.

47
00:02:49,755 --> 00:02:52,361
Before we'd trust this on anything real,

48
00:02:52,361 --> 00:02:57,182
we tested it against questions where we
already know the correct outcome —

49
00:02:57,182 --> 00:02:58,420
fifty-nine of them,

50
00:02:58,420 --> 00:03:02,720
covering genuine gaps, duplicates, and
questions it should refuse.

51
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:08,127
That turns the question from ‘does this look
reasonable’ into ‘did it get the right

52
00:03:08,127 --> 00:03:10,473
answer,’ which is a much higher bar,

53
00:03:10,473 --> 00:03:12,688
and the one that actually matters.

54
00:03:13,988 --> 00:03:18,003
Here's what that discipline looks like in the
actual code.

55
00:03:18,003 --> 00:03:23,196
Every answer the system drafts has to carry
its source, a confidence score,

56
00:03:23,196 --> 00:03:27,350
and a stated reason — or the object simply
can't be created.

57
00:03:27,350 --> 00:03:30,465
This isn't a guideline we hope gets
followed.

58
00:03:30,465 --> 00:03:35,727
It's enforced in the data structure itself,
before a draft can exist at all.

59
00:03:37,027 --> 00:03:40,478
And this is what comes out the other side
after a run.

60
00:03:40,478 --> 00:03:45,271
One reviewable decision per gap, the source
right there next to the answer,

61
00:03:45,271 --> 00:03:50,511
ready for a person to check in under a minute
— instead of researching and writing

62
00:03:50,511 --> 00:03:51,711
it from nothing.

63
00:03:52,833 --> 00:03:55,166
Here's the part that matters most,

64
00:03:55,166 --> 00:04:00,793
and the part worth remembering above
everything else in this video: EverMind never

65
00:04:00,793 --> 00:04:02,097
publishes anything.

66
00:04:02,097 --> 00:04:03,297
Not once.

67
00:04:03,297 --> 00:04:08,787
Not automatically, and not under any
configuration or setting you could turn on.

68
00:04:08,787 --> 00:04:12,191
There is no version of this where it skips
the human.

69
00:04:13,141 --> 00:04:17,314
And it isn't a policy we're asking you to
trust on good faith.

70
00:04:17,314 --> 00:04:20,544
There's no path in the code that lets it
happen.

71
00:04:20,544 --> 00:04:26,063
The only thing the system is capable of
writing is one list, for a person to read.

72
00:04:26,063 --> 00:04:31,379
Reaching your actual knowledge base from
there requires a separate, deliberate,

73
00:04:31,379 --> 00:04:33,600
human action — every single time.

74
00:04:34,900 --> 00:04:36,351
One more trust point,

75
00:04:36,351 --> 00:04:41,740
because this question always comes up: what
if someone tries to manipulate it?

76
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:47,474
Every logged question is treated strictly as
text to read — never as an instruction

77
00:04:47,474 --> 00:04:48,787
the system follows.

78
00:04:48,787 --> 00:04:51,965
If someone embeds a command inside a
question,

79
00:04:51,965 --> 00:04:56,318
the worst case is a rejected draft a human
discards in seconds.

80
00:04:56,318 --> 00:05:00,671
There's no path from a clever question to an
unreviewed action.

81
00:05:01,971 --> 00:05:05,086
So here's how it actually reaches a
customer.

82
00:05:05,086 --> 00:05:10,071
A person reads the drafted decision, checks
the cited source themselves,

83
00:05:10,071 --> 00:05:12,424
and decides whether to approve it.

84
00:05:12,424 --> 00:05:17,893
That's the only way anything this system
finds ever reaches the knowledge base,

85
00:05:17,893 --> 00:05:19,763
and from there, a customer.

86
00:05:21,063 --> 00:05:23,1000
Fifty-nine questions, one team, one month.

87
00:05:23,1000 --> 00:05:27,845
That's not a one-time cleanup — it repeats
every month,

88
00:05:27,845 --> 00:05:32,391
and it repeats on every team you have that
runs a knowledge base.

89
00:05:32,391 --> 00:05:37,216
The twenty-minute tax from the start of this
video isn't an incident.

90
00:05:37,216 --> 00:05:39,943
It's the ongoing cost of doing nothing.

91
00:05:41,243 --> 00:05:43,931
And we want to be upfront about its limit,

92
00:05:43,931 --> 00:05:49,115
because every tool that sounds this capable
eventually gets asked to do more than

93
00:05:49,115 --> 00:05:50,315
it should.

94
00:05:50,315 --> 00:05:54,923
When EverMind can't find a real source to
support an answer, it says so.

95
00:05:54,923 --> 00:05:59,467
It doesn't guess, and it doesn't fill the gap
with something plausible.

96
00:05:59,467 --> 00:06:04,715
An answer with no evidence behind it isn't
treated as a weak answer — it's treated

97
00:06:04,715 --> 00:06:05,915
as an invalid one,

98
00:06:05,915 --> 00:06:08,153
and it never reaches a reviewer at all.

99
00:06:09,103 --> 00:06:13,124
This is a working project, built from real
support questions,

100
00:06:13,124 --> 00:06:18,200
with every decision it makes already defined
and already tested against known

101
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:20,507
answers — not a concept on a slide.

102
00:06:20,507 --> 00:06:24,265
We're not asking you to change how your team
works today.

103
00:06:24,265 --> 00:06:29,011
We're asking for the chance to run this
against one real knowledge base,

104
00:06:29,011 --> 00:06:31,451
and show you the twenty minutes back.

105
00:06:31,451 --> 00:06:32,651
That's EverMind.
